Vulkan: a multisampled render target is resolved by its render pass (a resolve attachment; a depth through render
pass 2 where the device can), and a view's BGFX_CLEAR_DISCARD_* is honoured for the whole render pass it ends.

bgfx stored every multisampled colour attachment and resolved it after the pass with vkCmdResolveImage, which
reads all the samples back. On a tile GPU (Adreno, Mali, PowerVR) that is the full multisampled picture written to
memory and read again each frame: the 4x scene and the UI's 2x display-size layers. The single-sample image of
such a texture is now a resolve attachment of the frame buffer (VkSubpassDescription::pResolveAttachments): the
resolve happens on chip, and the samples are not written at all when the view that ends the pass discards them
(store op DONT_CARE). FrameBufferVK::resolve() then copies nothing (TextureVK::m_resolvedInPass); the mips of an
auto-mip target are still made. Not for a view in another format (sRGB: resolveRenderPass converts) or a layered
attachment; a multisampled colour without a resolve attachment keeps its samples whatever the view discards.

A multisampled depth that is sampled after its pass (the scene depth the engine carries to the display size at a
render scale) had its samples stored, and resolveDepth drew sample 0 of each into the single-sample image in a pass
of its own whenever the frame buffer was left. Where the device has VK_KHR_depth_stencil_resolve (with
VK_KHR_create_renderpass2) or Vulkan 1.2, where both are core whether or not the extensions are listed, and a
sample-zero resolve of depth and stencil, its render passes are made with vkCreateRenderPass2(KHR) and a
depth/stencil resolve attachment (VK_RESOLVE_MODE_SAMPLE_ZERO_BIT: the sample
resolveDepth's shader reads), the single-sample image is that attachment while the frame buffer is bound, and
resolve() draws nothing. getVkDepthResolve says which path the device takes and why; STORM_VK_DEPTH_RESOLVE_IN_PASS=0
in the environment at init keeps resolveDepth (the host check of that path). A multisampled depth resolved after its
pass keeps its samples whatever the view discards. Not for a read-only depth. resolveDepth's own pass writes every
pixel's depth and so clears the single-sample image instead of loading it.

bgfx keeps one render pass open through consecutive views into the same frame buffer and rect, and took its load
and store ops from the view that began it: a discard on the last of those views never reached the store op. The
pass now takes the discard flags of the last view it will run (looked ahead over the sorted draws: the same frame
buffer and rect, no blit before it; a compute dispatch inside ends the pass early, so nothing is discarded then).
